\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces \color  {DodgerBlue3} \sffamily  \fontsize  {9}{10}\selectfont  \textbf  {Supplementary FILD/FILA robustness and secondary diagnostics.} (a) ZeBRA predicts \textit  {MUC5B} carrier status at chance level in pooled and disease-stratified analyses, showing that the clinical score does not reconstruct the focal genotype. (b) Empirical high-probability residual-genomic scales across the nine prespecified direct-LR estimators; the 95\% and 99\% scales are stable while the 99.5\% tail is more estimator sensitive. (c) Genomic-induced decision changes remain localized near the clinical LR boundary. (d) Operational FPR differences for the matched-FPR rescue rules are near zero. (e) Held-out sensitivity at stringent requested FPRs complements the LR$+$ comparison in main Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref {fig:integrated-fild}g. (f) The explicitly adjudicated-negative rescue diagnostic is directionally favorable for most wider rescue bands but is underpowered because the adjudicated-negative subset is small. \relax }}{17}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:supp-integrated}{{S1}{17}{\color {DodgerBlue3} \sffamily \fontsize {9}{10}\selectfont \textbf {Supplementary FILD/FILA robustness and secondary diagnostics.} (a) ZeBRA predicts \textit {MUC5B} carrier status at chance level in pooled and disease-stratified analyses, showing that the clinical score does not reconstruct the focal genotype. (b) Empirical high-probability residual-genomic scales across the nine prespecified direct-LR estimators; the 95\% and 99\% scales are stable while the 99.5\% tail is more estimator sensitive. (c) Genomic-induced decision changes remain localized near the clinical LR boundary. (d) Operational FPR differences for the matched-FPR rescue rules are near zero. (e) Held-out sensitivity at stringent requested FPRs complements the LR$+$ comparison in main Fig.~\ref {fig:integrated-fild}g. (f) The explicitly adjudicated-negative rescue diagnostic is directionally favorable for most wider rescue bands but is underpowered because the adjudicated-negative subset is small. \relax }{figure.caption.7}{}}
